# Line endings.
#
# 120 text files here were committed with CRLF and 7 with LF, with nothing
# declaring which was intended. The visible cost was that every diff between
# two refs looked like a whole-file rewrite: `git diff v0.4.0..main` reported
# 15,000 changed lines, while `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol v0.4.0..main`
# reported roughly 150. A reviewer (or a release-time `git diff --exit-code`)
# could not tell a real change from a re-saved file.
#
# Normalising to LF in the repository fixes that at the source. `text=auto`
# lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` pins the checkout so a Windows clone
# produces the same bytes as a macOS one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The payloads. `binary` is git's macro for `-text -diff`: never convert these,
# never try to diff them as lines.
#
# This is not cosmetic. Every entry in data/inventory.json carries a sha256 of
# its .splat file, test_files_match_their_checksums_and_counts verifies each
# one, and python/src/splatset/_fetch.py re-checks the digest of every download.
# One byte rewritten in transit invalidates the checksum, which breaks loading
# for every user rather than failing a build. The .png thumbnails are likewise
# compressed streams where a line-ending translation would corrupt the image.
*.splat binary
*.png   binary

# Stated explicitly rather than relying on auto-detection, so the intent
# survives a future edit to the rules above.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# A zero-byte marker file with no extension: auto-detection cannot classify it,
# so say so here or it shows up as churn on every platform.
py.typed text eol=lf
